A provider that is not on any registry — one you build from source in the same workspace — is a terraform_provider whose files is the built binary. It is used exactly like a hub-provided provider: load("@rules_go//go:def.bzl", "go_binary")
load("@rules_terraform//terraform:defs.bzl", "terraform_module", "terraform_provider") go_binary( name = "terraform-provider-custom", # the engine requires this prefix srcs = ["main.go"],
) terraform_provider( name = "custom", files = [":terraform-provider-custom"], source = "example/custom", # matches `required_providers` version = "1.0.0",
) terraform_module( name = "app", srcs = glob(["*.tf"]), lock = ".terraform.lock.hcl", deps = [":custom"],
) Leave platform unset: it is read from the configuration the provider is built in, so the same target works on a Linux CI runner and an Apple Silicon laptop. The .terraform.lock.hcl still needs an entry for the provider — write one by hand with the version and a placeholder h1: hash; the init aspect replaces the h1: with the hash of the binary it actually installed. A module that depends on providers but has no lock is an analysis error, since the engine will not use a provider directory it cannot check against one.

13.body:
There is no lock file for registry modules — the extension resolves them live against the Registry API on every fresh evaluation. Terraform users load the extension from the terraform side: terraform.deps( name = "shared", lock = "//:.terraform.lock.hcl", # Every directory whose `module` blocks name a registry module, root # modules and shared libraries alike, given as any .tf file inside it. roots = [ "//envs/prod/app:main.tf", "//modules/naming:main.tf", ],
) Then every terraform_module whose blocks name a registry module depends on @shared (or @shared//:modules for a library that should not install providers). Archives are pooled, and a module depending on the hub depends on a candidate pool from which only what its own module blocks name is installed, so a tag may list many directories. The monorepo example does this across three environments and a shared library. OpenTofu users load the parallel extension — same tag class, but the registry defaults to registry.opentofu.org: tofu = use_extension("@rules_terraform//tofu:extensions.bzl", "tofu")
tofu.deps( name = "app", lock = "//:.terraform.lock.hcl", roots = ["//:main.tf"],
)
use_repo(tofu, "app") At extension eval time the impl reads every *.tf file in each directory listed in roots (matching Terraform's own "root module is a directory" model), parses module { source = "…" } blocks, and for every registry-shaped source: Hits /v1/modules/<ns>/<name>/<provider>/versions and picks the highest version satisfying the block's version constraint via a Starlark port of Terraform's semver logic. Hits /v1/modules/<ns>/<name>/<provider>/<version> for metadata — reads source (git URL) and tag, constructs a direct GitHub tarball URL (<source>/archive/refs/tags/<tag>.tar.gz) with the matching strip_prefix. Downloads the archive to compute the sha256. Once every modules tag has been resolved, registers one http_archive per distinct (registry, module address, version) in a pool shared by every hub (rt_mod_<registry>_<address>_<version>), extracted whole. Each //subdir any hub referenced is a target inside that one repository rather than a second download. The hub itself is a terraform_module_group over the pool targets it resolved to. Every downstream fetch goes direct to GitHub — no registry hop at build time. Only GitHub-backed modules are supported; non-GitHub git hosts fail with a clear error at eval time. A source written with its own hostname (app.terraform.io/acme/vpc/aws) is resolved against that host rather than the tag's registry. Modules the extension cannot fetch — a registry module hosted off GitHub, or any plain git:: / https:// source — can still be supplied by hand. Fetch the archive with whatever repository rule fits, declare a terraform_external_module in its build file with source copied verbatim from the module block, and put the target in deps: # MODULE.bazel
http_archive = use_repo_rule("@bazel_tools//tools/build_defs/repo:http.bzl", "http_archive")
http_archive( name = "acme_vpc", urls = ["https://git.example.com/acme/terraform-vpc/-/archive/v1.4.0/terraform-vpc-v1.4.0.tar.gz"], strip_prefix = "terraform-vpc-v1.4.0", integrity = "sha256-…", build_file_content = """
load("@rules_terraform//terraform:defs.bzl", "terraform_external_module")
terraform_external_module( name = "module", key = "acme_vpc", source = "git::https://git.example.com/acme/terraform-vpc.git?ref=v1.4.0", version = "1.4.0", srcs = glob(["**/*.tf"]), visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
)
""",
) The init aspect installs it under every module block whose source matches, the same way it places a registry module. At build time the init aspect copies each module's files into .terraform/modules/<key>/ and adds a corresponding entry to Terraform's modules.json manifest. Reproducibility and caching. The extension is reproducible = False and every .tf edit in the root module directory invalidates it (bzlmod tracks each module_ctx.read()). Re-eval cost is cached across runs via module_ctx.facts — but that cache only persists when MODULE.bazel.lock is enabled. See Reproducibility for the full recipe and the Implementation detail: network calls per eval note below. Non-default registries. Pass registry = "…" on the tag if you're pointing at a private mirror or an alternate registry; it applies to the providers and the modules of that tag alike. Implementation detail: network calls per eval For each module { } block, the extension issues up to three Registry API requests, cached across evals via module_ctx.facts: Call Purpose Cost Skipped when GET /v1/modules/<source>/versions Resolve version constraint to a concrete SemVer Small JSON version is already an exact-pinned SemVer GET /v1/modules/<source>/<version> Read source (git URL) + tag to build the archive URL Small JSON Facts cache has an entry for <source>@<version> GET <archive URL> Stream the archive body to compute an sha256 integrity MB per module Facts cache has an entry for <source>@<version> Facts is keyed by <source>@<concrete_version> and stores {url, strip_prefix, integrity} — historical facts that don't change once a module version is published. Only entries for currently-declared modules carry across evals; entries for removed modules prune naturally.

14.body:
Modules living inside your monorepo don't need a lock file — Bazel's own target graph is the source of truth. Give the child its own terraform_module and list it in the parent's deps. The path lives in one place — the module block — and deps just says which target supplies it, the way a py_library dep supplies an import: # root/main.tf
module "greeter" { source = "./modules/greeter"
} terraform_module( name = "root", srcs = glob(["*.tf"]), deps = ["//root/modules/greeter"],
) When the child's package sits under the parent's — the usual case, //root/modules/greeter beneath //root — the aspect places it at modules/greeter inside the module directory, which is already what source = "./modules/greeter" names. When it doesn't, the source path is matched against the tail of each dep's package. A child at //shared/greeter satisfies source = "./greeter"; nothing changes in the BUILD file: terraform_module( name = "root", srcs = glob(["*.tf"]), deps = ["//shared/greeter"],
) Two mistakes are build errors rather than a silently wrong module directory: a local source no dep supplies, and a terraform_module dep no module block references. If two deps could satisfy the same source, that's an error too — rename one directory or nest it under the parent's package.

